  "seed": 42,
  "series": "DEBUGGING RUNNING CONTAINERS",
  "date": "15/09/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "The Debugging Mindset",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What is a container?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A container = a process wrapped in namespaces + cgroups. Debugging containers is really debugging a process with an isolated view."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No init system, no systemd usually",
            "Shared kernel with the host",
            "`PID 1` is your app/entrypoint",
            "No zombie reaper unless `--init`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Debug surface layers",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "exec: process",
              "logs: stdio",
              "healthchecks",
              "cgroups: resources",
              "namespaces: isolation"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Golden rule",
          "text": "Never hand-fix a running container. Reproduce, fix the image/config, redeploy — a 'fixed' container is a drift bomb that resets on restart."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Read the STATUS column",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Status",
            "Meaning",
            "Move"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Up",
              "running but broken",
              "`exec` + logs"
            ],
            [
              "Restarting",
              "crash loop",
              "`logs -p`"
            ],
            [
              "Exited",
              "dead on arrival",
              "`ps -a`, exit code"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Exit codes diagnose: `137` = SIGKILL (OOM), `143` = SIGTERM",
            "`126/127` = command/exec problem",
            "`docker ps -a` shows exited; `start`/`attach` re-enters"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Is it the container, the orchestrator, or the host?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`docker inspect` → container layer",
            "`kubectl describe` → orchestrator",
            "`dmesg` → host/kernel",
            "Ephemeral FS: persist state via volumes first"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "docker inspect -f '{{.State.ExitCode}}' ctr",
            "dmesg | grep -i oom"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Toolkit map",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "docker logs",
              "docker exec",
              "docker inspect",
              "docker stats",
              "nsenter /proc"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Cattle, not pets",
          "items": [
            "Immutable images only",
            "Changes vanish on restart",
            "Evidence first, surgery last"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "docker exec: Into the Live Process",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What exec really does",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`docker exec` spawns a NEW process inside the container's existing namespaces. It never 'enters' PID 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec -it web sh   # prefer sh over bash",
            "-i: keep STDIN open",
            "-t: allocate a TTY",
            "# no -t in scripts; fine without colors"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "exec vs attach",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "attach",
                "lines": [
                  "binds to PID 1 stdio",
                  "Ctrl-C can kill app"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "exec",
                "lines": [
                  "side process",
                  "safer for debugging"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Env inherited from config; `exec -e VAR=x` probes env bugs",
            "`--user 1000:1000` tests permission issues",
            "exec'd procs show in `docker top` + host `ps`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "No shell in the image (distroless)?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`docker debug` (podman)",
            "`kubectl debug` ephemeral containers",
            "`docker cp` a static busybox in"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kubectl debug -it pod --image=busybox"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scriptable probes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec web ps aux",
            "docker exec web env",
            "docker exec web cat /etc/resolv.conf",
            "docker exec web test -f /tmp/ready  # exit = probe"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Write permission test",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec web touch /data/probe \\",
            "  && docker exec web rm /data/probe",
            "echo $?   # 0 = volume writable"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "exec fails on crash-looping containers — the process table never settles. Read logs, or run an identical container on the same volume and debug that."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "One-liners",
          "items": [
            "`ps aux` — what's running",
            "`env` — config sanity",
            "`test -f` — scriptable health"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Logs: stdout, stderr, Drivers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where logs live",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Docker captures fd 1 + fd 2 as JSON lines via the `json-file` driver, under `/var/lib/docker/containers/<id>/<id>-json.log`."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker logs -f web      # like tail -f",
            "docker logs --tail 100 web",
            "docker logs --since 30m web",
            "docker logs -t web  # -t adds timestamps"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-t` exposes lag between log time and real event time",
            "Nonzero exit codes land in state, not logs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Classic trap",
          "text": "Files written inside the container (`/var/log/nginx`) never reach `docker logs`. Symlink them to `/dev/stdout`, or run `tail -F` to forward."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Driver gotchas",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Driver",
            "docker logs?",
            "Tool"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "json-file",
              "yes",
              "`docker logs`"
            ],
            [
              "journald",
              "no",
              "`journalctl`"
            ],
            [
              "fluentd",
              "no",
              "collector UI"
            ],
            [
              "syslog",
              "no",
              "log server"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Default driver has NO size limit — set `--log-opt max-size`/`max-file` or the disk fills silently",
            "Long lines split with `'partial': true` markers",
            "Stack traces: handle multiline in the collector, not the source"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Crash loop — how to see the dead run?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`kubectl logs <pod> -c <ctr> -p`",
            "`-p` = previous crashed instance",
            "Gold for loop diagnosis"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kubectl logs web -c app -p --tail=50"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Joining logs",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Log container ID/hostname at startup",
            "Log request ID to join app ↔ infra logs",
            "Trade-off: stdout = central, loses file structure — pick per component"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "app stdout",
              "json-file",
              "collector",
              "store / alert"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Healthchecks: Broken vs Alive",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Define a probe",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f \\\\"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
